title: DEPENDENCY_<ID> defined for unresolved optional deps (define-set mismatch with game)
updated_at: "2026-08-29T13:30:16Z"
---
Found by the B013 lane (GH #58) while building the namespace-rule authority
guard, 2026-08-24. Pre-existing; independent of that rule.

## Defect
`LspConfig::apply_manifest` defines `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` for every dependency
DECLARED in info.toml, whether or not it actually resolved on this machine. The
game defines `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` only for dependencies it actually loaded. So for
an uninstalled optional dependency the LSP compiles `#if DEPENDENCY_X` branches
that the real game masks out — a define-set mismatch with the game, in the
direction that makes dead code live.

## Consequences
1. Code guarded by `#if DEPENDENCY_X` is checked against a workspace that does
   NOT contain X's symbols, so anything it references is unresolvable. That is a
   false-positive generator in its own right.
2. It forces the GH #58 namespace rule to disarm on any plugin with an
   uninstalled optional dep, because the pooled table cannot be authoritative
   while `#if`-guarded code referencing an absent module is live. Measured
   effect: `tests/fixtures/tm-dips-plus-plus` is disarmed entirely for this
   reason — a force-armed run reproduces the baseline error set exactly, so the
   rule is correct there but silent.

## Fix
Define `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` only for dependencies that resolved. Then the `#if`
branch state matches the game, and the namespace rule (and anything else gated
on `dependency_closure_complete`) regains authority on plugins whose only gap
was an uninstalled optional dep.

## Why NOT done on the GH #58 branch (decision)
It changes the DEFINE SET, which changes which code gets checked, which can move
the FP fixture baselines (tm-editor-plus-plus 2 errors / 1428 warnings,
tm-dips-plus-plus 1 error / 258 warnings, tm-archivist 2/28, tm-counter 4/2,
tm-dashboard 4/5). That is its own red/green + dogfood exercise with its own
blast radius, not a rider on a diagnostic-rule branch. Interacts with the GH #36
define ladder and the GH #57 define matrix.

## Work
- Gate the `DEPENDENCY_<ID>` define on actual resolution in `apply_manifest`.
- Decide what the define matrix should do with an unresolvable dep axis: it is
  arguably still worth checking BOTH states of `DEPENDENCY_X` (the plugin ships
  to users who do have X), which is exactly what `define_matrix` is for — so the
  fix may be "don't define it by default, but let the matrix toggle it".
- Re-evaluate the four-condition authority guard on
  `PluginWorkspaceLoad::dependency_closure_complete`: this fix should let
  condition (optional/transitive deps unresolved) stop costing coverage on
  plugins that merely lack an optional dep locally.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: a plugin declaring an OPTIONAL dependency that is not installed, with
`#if DEPENDENCY_X` code referencing X's API → today the branch is live and the
references are unresolvable; after the fix the branch is masked and the file is
clean. Variants: required dep missing (already reported separately — must not
regress); dep resolved → define present and branch live; `define_matrix` axis
explicitly toggling `DEPENDENCY_X` on for an uninstalled dep (must still work,
per the decision above); a dep resolved from an `.op` archive; transitive
`export_dependencies` unresolved. Also assert the GH #58 namespace rule re-arms
on tm-dips-plus-plus once its only gap is gone, and that its error set matches
the force-armed baseline recorded on the B013 branch.

## Decision (coordinator, 2026-08-29) — matrix axis for unresolved deps

Do NOT define DEPENDENCY_<ID> for a dependency that did not resolve. The
define_matrix MUST still be able to toggle it on explicitly (an axis naming
DEPENDENCY_X is honoured even when X is uninstalled) — the plugin ships to
users who do have X, and checking both branch states is exactly what the
matrix is for. So: not defined by default, matrix-toggleable.

Required deps that fail to resolve keep their current behaviour (reported
separately) — do not regress that path.

## Decision (coordinator) — where resolution is known

apply_manifest currently sees only &Manifest, which has no resolution info.
Either pass the resolved-dependency set in, or move/gate the DEPENDENCY_ define
insertion to the point where resolution is known. Prefer passing an explicit
resolved-set argument so the function stays pure and testable.

## Sequencing (coordinator)

Runs SERIAL after B017 in the same worktree — both edit src/config.rs.
Expect FP fixture baselines to move; record the deltas and explain each.

## Authority settled (lane research, 2026-08-29) — mapping rule + spec citation

Openplanet docs (Plugin Dependencies tutorial) state the rule verbatim:
'The definition will have a prefix of DEPENDENCY_ followed by the plugin ID in
uppercase, with spaces, dashes, and periods replaced by underscores.'
Same page defines plugin ID as the folder name, or the file base name of a .op.

Corroborated against the real plugin corpus (~/src/openplanet/my-plugins,
~/OpenplanetNext/Plugins): tm-control-mcp -> DEPENDENCY_TM_CONTROL_MCP,
ghosts-pp -> DEPENDENCY_GHOSTS_PP, map-together -> DEPENDENCY_MAP_TOGETHER,
ManiaExchange -> DEPENDENCY_MANIAEXCHANGE. No real dependency ID uses +, @, /
or non-ASCII.

### Coordinator ruling: match the docs EXACTLY, do not superset

Replace ONLY space, '-' and '.'; uppercase; leave every other character
untouched. The current implementation replaces every non-ASCII-alphanumeric
with '_', which is NOT a safe superset:

  ID 'Dips++' -> docs/game define DEPENDENCY_DIPS++ (inert: no #if can
  reference it, the lexer ends the identifier at '+')
  ID 'Dips++' -> current impl defines DEPENDENCY_DIPS__ (a WELL-FORMED
  identifier the game never defines)

The superset converts the game's inert names into reachable ones the game
lacks -- exactly the B019 failure mode (LSP has a define the game does not,
making dead code live), reintroduced inside the function B019 is fixing.
Test must assert DEPENDENCY_DIPS++ IS produced and DEPENDENCY_DIPS__ is NOT.

Collision (my_plugin / my-plugin / my.plugin all -> MY_PLUGIN) is Openplanet's
own and must be reproduced, NOT diverged from. Warn when two declared deps of
one manifest collapse to the same suffix: signal without divergence.
Leading-digit IDs are a non-issue (the DEPENDENCY_ prefix means the leading
char is 'D'). Empty/whitespace-only ID -> skip and warn, never emit a bare
DEPENDENCY_.

Comment at dependency_define_suffix must mark which part is authority (the
docs rule) and which is inference (behaviour for chars outside {space,-,.}
is doc-specified but unverified against the running game).

## The repo's own spec already mandates this fix

specs/draft-v1.md:190 -- 'For optional_dependencies, if not found, define no
DEPENDENCY_X preprocessor symbol; if found, define it'
specs/draft-v1.md:260 -- 'Plus DEPENDENCY_X for found optional dependencies'
(also restated at 4.1 step 5 / 6.4)

Verified by the coordinator against specs/draft-v1.md on master. So the
current behaviour VIOLATES the repo's written spec; this task restores
documented behaviour rather than changing it, and the FP baseline movement is
a spec-conformance correction, not a judgment call. Cite this in the commit.

## CORRECTION: the FP baselines quoted above are STALE (coordinator, 2026-08-30)

The counts cited in this ticket (tm-editor-plus-plus 2/1428, tm-dips-plus-plus
1/258, tm-archivist 2/28, tm-counter 4/2, tm-dashboard 4/5) are WRONG. They
were carried forward from older tickets and never re-measured.

Measured independently by the coordinator on 2026-08-30, comparing a master
build against the B016 branch build, full check output byte-diffed:

  tm-editor-plus-plus   14 errors / 1485 warnings
  tm-dips-plus-plus      5 errors /  276 warnings
  tm-archivist           2 errors /   35 warnings
  tm-counter             4 errors /    2 warnings
  tm-dashboard           4 errors /    4 warnings

Only tm-counter matched the stale numbers. The error counts on the two big
fixtures were off by 12 and 4.

Independently corroborated: the B016 lane built a binary from a genuinely
archived 91a296c tree and reached the same five pairs.

### Consequence for anyone measuring a define-set change

Do NOT gate against the numbers written in a ticket. Build a baseline binary
from the archived base commit, run the fixtures with it, and compare against
your branch's binary. Byte-diff the full check output rather than comparing
counts — identical counts can hide a swapped diagnostic.

Trap seen in practice: a review agent produced 'baseline' numbers from a
binary that was NOT built from the base commit (it accepted an identifier the
base rejects). Verify what tree a comparison binary came from before trusting
its numbers.

Command used:
  ./target/debug/openplanet-lsp check --typedb-dir tests/fixtures/typedb \
      tests/fixtures/<fixture>